{"name":"Salt Spray","hex":"#a7c5ce","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"salt spray","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Seawater in the form of a fine mist or droplets."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/salt_spray"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sea spray","blocks":[{"text":"Sea spray consists of aerosol particles formed from the ocean, primarily by ejection into Earth's atmosphere through bursting bubbles at the air-sea interface. Sea spray contains both organic matter and inorganic salts that form sea salt aerosol (SSA). SSA has the ability to form cloud condensation nuclei (CCN) and remove anthropogenic aerosol pollutants from the atmosphere. Coarse sea spray has also been found to inhibit the development of lightning in storm clouds."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Sea_spray_and_waves_%28Unsplash%29.jpg/330px-Sea_spray_and_waves_%28Unsplash%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sea_spray","wikidata":"Q1046888"},{"title":"Salt","blocks":[{"text":"Salt is a mineral composed primarily of sodium chloride (NaCl). When used in food, especially in granulated form, it is more formally called table salt. In the form of a natural crystalline mineral, salt is also known as rock salt or halite. Salt is essential for life in general, and saltiness is one of the basic human tastes. Salt is one of the oldest and most ubiquitous food seasonings, and is known to uniformly improve the taste perception of food. Salting, brining, and pickling are ancient and important methods of food preservation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Rock_salt_%28halitite%29_%28Billianwala_Salt_Member%2C_Salt_Range_Formation%2C_Ediacaran_to_Lower_Cambrian%3B_Khewra_Salt_Mine%2C_Salt_Range%2C_Pakistan%29_14.jpg/330px-Rock_salt_%28halitite%29_%28Billianwala_Salt_Member%2C_Salt_Range_Formation%2C_Ediacaran_to_Lower_Cambrian%3B_Khewra_Salt_Mine%2C_Salt_Range%2C_Pakistan%29_14.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salt","wikidata":"Q11254"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Sea_spray_sea_foam_connection.png/500px-Sea_spray_sea_foam_connection.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Sea_spray_sea_foam_connection.png/1280px-Sea_spray_sea_foam_connection.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Sea_spray_sea_foam_connection.png/500px-Sea_spray_sea_foam_connection.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Connection between sea foam and sea spray formation. The dark orange line indicates processes common to the formation of both sea spray and sea foam.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASea_spray_sea_foam_connection.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Ocean_mist_and_spray_2.jpg/500px-Ocean_mist_and_spray_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Ocean_mist_and_spray_2.jpg/1280px-Ocean_mist_and_spray_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Ocean_mist_and_spray_2.jpg/500px-Ocean_mist_and_spray_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":" Sea spray containing marine microorganisms can be swept high into the atmosphere where they become aeroplankton. These airborne microorganisms may travel the globe before falling back to earth.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOcean_mist_and_spray_2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thu